From 0638fd56ff381dd914a551c6cd376b1bef0f187d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:55:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08065/11362] [outer-loop] document that gVisor GPU passthrough
 is unusable on this host

Operator question while reviewing the sandbox-routing fix: "We don't want to
impact GPU activity by using gvisor - is it compatible?" Measured rather than
assumed:

    host NVIDIA driver          610.43.03  (2x RTX 3090)
    runsc release-20260330.0    nvproxy supports up to 590.48.01
    nvidia-container-toolkit    NOT INSTALLED
    carnot-sandbox:latest       NOT BUILT
    DockerSandboxConfig.gpu     False (default)

gVisor does not pass GPU calls to the driver directly -- it intercepts them via
nvproxy, which whitelists specific driver versions. 610.43.03 is newer than
anything this runsc build recognises, and the toolkit providing --gpus is absent
regardless of runtime. So gpu=True + runtime="runsc" fails twice over here.

No impact on the project's GPU work, which is the operator's actual concern: the
sandbox runs LLM-generated hypothesis code (CPU JAX), while training and
inference go through separate paths (llama_cpp, torch) that never touch this
module. The routing change committed in 43897dbbfc also leaves the default path
byte-identical -- with neither env var set, execution is in-process and Docker is
never probed, asserted by test_does_not_touch_docker_by_default.

CLAUDE.md's Security Requirements said gVisor "plays nicely with
nvidia-container-toolkit". True of gVisor in general, not actionable on this host
at these versions. Added as a dated CORRECTION beneath the original claim rather
than editing it, per the never-prune rule -- the general statement is still
right, it just needed the local measurement attached.

Documented in three places, chosen so it is found at the moment it matters: the
constants block in sandbox_docker.py (where someone would flip gpu=True),
ops/known-issues.md (with the re-check condition), and CLAUDE.md.

Records the escape hatch too: for isolation WITH GPU, use runtime="runc" plus
nvidia-container-toolkit -- keeps the GPU and namespace/network/filesystem
isolation, drops the gVisor syscall boundary. And explicitly: do NOT downgrade
the driver to fit nvproxy's supported range.
